//- 1:15 WORDS THAT DON'T TRANSLATE, then 2:30 REDHEADS. The first evidence for the thesis: if color were out in the world, languages would agree on where each one starts and stops.

//- Paint chips: the colour, its word, and what the other language needs to say it. Eight a slide, four by two. The hex values are guesses, in scss/talk/_swatches.scss.
section.swatches(data-timing="30")
  h2 Only in Spanish
  dl
    .celeste
      dt(lang="es") celeste
      dd sky blue
    .trigueno
      dt(lang="es") trigueño
      dd wheat-colored, of skin or hair
    .zarco
      dt(lang="es") zarco
      dd light blue, of eyes
    .moreno
      dt(lang="es") moreno
      dd tanned, or dark-haired
    .canoso
      dt(lang="es") canoso
      dd gray-haired
    .blanquiazul
      dt(lang="es") blanquiazul
      dd white-and-blue (Málaga CF)
    .pardo
      dt(lang="es") pardo
      dd gray-brown, like every cat at night
    .amoratado
      dt(lang="es") amoratado
      dd purple, of bruises and cold lips
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Evidence: if color were out in the world, languages would agree on where each one starts and stops.
      li Colors of particular things: skin, eyes, hair, sky. English needs a phrase for every one.
      li Read three or four. The rest make the point by being there.
      li Moreno is what everyone on the beach out there is trying to get.
      li Blanquiazul: Spanish welds two colors into one word. Málaga CF's colors.
      li Pardo: Spanish says every cat at night is pardo. English says they're all gray.
      li Unverified: check every word with a native speaker from Spain before it ships.

section.swatches(data-timing="30")
  h2 Only in English
  dl
    .teal
      dt teal
      dd(lang="es") verde azulado
    .hazel
      dt hazel
      dd(lang="es") color avellana
    .auburn
      dt auburn
      dd(lang="es") castaño rojizo
    .navy
      dt navy
      dd(lang="es") azul marino
    .taupe
      dt taupe
      dd(lang="es") gris topo
    .eggshell
      dt eggshell
      dd(lang="es") blanco cáscara de huevo
    .periwinkle
      dt periwinkle
      dd(lang="es") azul pervinca
    .puce
      dt puce
      dd(lang="es") color pulga
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Spanish needs two words for every one of these. Eggshell takes four.
      li Taupe is French for mole, and topo is Spanish for mole. Puce is French for flea, and half the English speakers here don't know it either.
      li Unverified, azul pervinca and color pulga most of all.

section.statement(data-timing="15")
  h2 What does that say about us?
  p.aside (hold that thought)
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Leave it hanging. The denouement answers it.

//- 2:30 REDHEADS: the words drift over time too, and orange is barely a word, which hands straight to brown. docs/TALK.md, Claims, has the dates.
section.statement(data-timing="15")
  h2 Orange is named after the fruit.
  p English had the fruit for a century before anyone called a color orange.
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Evidence: the words drift over time too.
      li The fruit is in English by about 1380. The first "orenge colour" is in Elizabeth of York's wardrobe accounts in 1502.
      li Before that, red stretched to cover it.

section.statement(data-timing="15")
  h2 That's why redheads are red.
  p.aside.fragment (so are robin redbreasts. they're fossils.)
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Red for hair is late Old English, and "redhead" goes back to 1256.
      li Never say red fox or red squirrel: both were named after orange existed.

section.statement(data-timing="10")
  h2 Spanish files them under red too.
  p(lang="es") pelirrojo, petirrojo
  p.aside.fragment (the RAE defines red hair as "a very bright blond")
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Don't say Spanish lacked the word. It had naranjado by 1351.
      li Petirrojo is peto (bib) plus rojo, not pecho.
      li Check the DLE's entry for rojo before saying the blond line. It came from a search snippet.
